From 7a49e42f3adbcfd405288655b4f9e84a92f21c43 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vela <vela@localhost>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 23:41:55 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] feat(belief): evidence provenance in both directions
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A belief's evidence trail said what was observed, never on which records the
observation rested. The citation lived in a free-text `source` string, so the
chain "which experience carries this evidence" broke exactly where an audit
needs it. This adds the missing half in both directions: which records (and
which revision of each) a belief rests on, and which beliefs depend on a
given record.

Storage is normalised — `belief_evidence_sources`, one row per
(belief, observation, record). Two parallel lists of rids and revision numbers
inside the belief payload would drift apart the first time one is written
without the other; a row cannot.

Invariants and where they are pinned (engine/tests/belief_sources.rs):

- Observation identity stays separate from source identity. Several records
  under one `observation_id` are several citations of ONE observation. The
  trail keeps one entry; the weight stays in the payload.
- Provenance is not weight. Citing three records moves `log_odds` exactly as
  much as citing one (bit-identical confidence).
- A new record revision is not a new experience. When a cited record is
  corrected and re-asserted under a fresh `observation_id`, the call is folded
  into the observation that already cites that record and the result says so
  (`folded_into_observation`). Without this, fixing a typo in a source would
  double the confidence. A *different* record is unaffected and remains
  independent evidence.
- A citation is a historical fact. `source_revision` is the revision at
  citation time; a later `correct()` bumps the record and leaves the row
  alone. The read path reports `revision_drift` — a signal to review, not to
  disbelieve.
- A retracted source stays a source. `forget()` tombstones the record; the
  citation survives, the reverse lookup still finds the dependent beliefs, and
  confidence does not move.
- Re-citing the same (belief, observation, record) is idempotent.
- Evidence update and citations land in ONE transaction, so a half-written
  provenance cannot look complete. An unknown rid is rejected before any write.
- Beliefs without citations stay compatible: no rows, no behaviour change.

`persist_cognitive_node` keeps its signature; the row writer moved into a
private helper so the new path can write both tables inside one transaction
without acquiring the connection mutex twice.

Not included: `belief_assert_evidence_batch` gains no sources (one observation
per call is the contract here), and nothing reduces a belief's confidence when
a source is retracted — that decision is separate from making the dependency
visible.

Measured: 14 Rust tests; without the fold guard the re-citation test fails.
